        "thinking": "New error: `Dynamic require of \"node:events\" is not supported`. This is in pino's `lib/proto.js`. \n\nThe issue: `pino` uses CommonJS `require(\"node:events\")` somewhere in its code. When bundled as ESM with esbuild using `platform: \"neutral\"`, dynamic requires (`require(...)`) get wrapped in a helper function that tries to resolve modules. But `node:events` is marked as external and the CF Workers runtime provides it as an ES module, not a CommonJS module.\n\nThe problem: `require(\"node:events\")` is a CommonJS-style require, but in the ESM bundle, `node:events` is available as an ES module from the CF runtime. \n\nThe esbuild-generated helper can't dynamically import ES modules, so the real solution is to bundle `node:events` directly instead of leaving it external—that way pino's require calls will use the bundled EventEmitter rather than trying to load an external module. way that CJS `require()` can access it.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully - the issue is that esbuild's CJS-to-ESM transform creates `__require2` for CommonJS requires. For most modules, it works because the required module is bundled. But for external modules, `__require2` falls back to trying to use `import()` which isn't synchronous.\n\nThe solution: Don't mark `events` and `node:events` as external. Instead, let esbuild bundle them from the node_modules (the `events` polyfill that many tools include, or use the CF-provided one).\n\nBut in CF Workers, `events` is provided by nodejs_compat. If we don't mark it as external, esbuild needs to find it locally. In the workspace, `events` is available as a package in `node_modules` - most likely it's the browser polyfill from npm.\n\nI'm wondering if I should try using `platform: \"node\"` instead of `platform: \"neutral\"` in esbuild, since that might handle CJS/ESM interop better for Node.js built-ins and make `require()` calls work properly. Though that could break bundling for the CF Workers target. Another option is to mark the CF-provided builtins as NOT external and bundle them instead. I think bundling our own `events` module is fine even if there's duplication. Let me try removing `events` and `node:events` from the external list so they get bundled instead of treated as external references. The core issue is that when `pino`'s CommonJS code tries to require `node:events` dynamically inside the bundle, it fails because external ESM modules can't be dynamically required from CJS—but if `events` is bundled, that requirement will resolve correctly. Though I'm also wondering if I should leverage `pino`'s browser build instead, which might avoid this whole CommonJS-in-ESM problem.\n\nActually, stepping back—trying to bundle a Node.js server package like `pino` for Cloudflare Workers is fundamentally misaligned. Instead of fighting the bundler to include all of `pino`'s dependencies, I should create a pino-compatible shim that wraps `console.log` under the hood, so the server code's logging interface stays the same but uses the runtime's native logging.\n\nCreating a pino shim...",
